// This shader guarantees that the first triangle or quad is clipped to cover
// the screen, and that all other triangles / quads are completely clipped and
// discarded.
//
// The first 4 vertices generated by this are:
//
// (-1.0, -1.0)
// (3.0, -1.0)
// (-1.0, 3.0)
// (3.0, 3.0)
//
// The first triangle gets clipped into a quad covering the full clip space,
// (-1.0, -1.0) to (1.0, 1.0). We can see that further vertices have all x >= 3
// and therefore all lie outside of clip space.
//
// Why 4.0? It's a power of 2, so some hardware does that for free, and it
// ensures that the first triangle hypotenuse is well outside of clip space.
//
// Why cover the screen with a single triangle instead of two? It's because
// fragment / pixel shaders are executed in 2x2 quads, and the diagonal line
// between two triangles results in throwing away some invocations.
float2 coverScreen(uint vertexID) {
	return float2(vertexID & 1u, vertexID >> 1u) * 4.0 - 1.0;
}
